# What a fuzzing run produces, none of which is source.
#
# `artifacts/` is the input that crashed a target, and it is the one thing here
# that is NOT committed. The way to keep a crashing input is the rule in
# README.md: add it as a case in the stable counterpart, written out in the
# test, so it is checked by every run of the ordinary suite rather than only
# while somebody is fuzzing. A file sitting here is checked by nobody.
#
# `corpus/` IS committed, which task-2066's "Done means" asks for by name. It is
# the inputs libfuzzer kept because they reached new coverage, and it is where
# the next run starts from - a fuzzer given the corpus reaches in seconds what
# it spent the previous campaign finding. It costs 3.7 MB after one
# twenty-second campaign across sixteen targets, which is the price of not
# starting from nothing every time.
artifacts/
coverage/
target/
